{"name":"Basil Pesto","hex":"#529d6e","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"basil","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant (Ocimum basilicum).","The leaves of this plant used as a herb."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The angle to which a joiner's tool is ground away."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/basil"},{"term":"pesto","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sauce, especially for pasta, originating from the Genoa region in Italy, made from basil, garlic, pine nuts, olive oil and cheese (usually pecorino)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pesto"}],"entries":[{"title":"Basil","blocks":[{"text":"Basil, also called great basil, is a culinary herb of the family Lamiaceae (mints). It is a tender plant and is used in cuisines worldwide. In Western cuisine, the generic term \"basil\" refers to the variety also known as Genovese basil or sweet basil. Basil is native to tropical and subtopical Asia and Australasia from India and Sri Lanka east to southeastern China, Taiwan, and south through Southeast Asia, the Philippines, New Guinea, to northern Australia. In temperate climates basil is treated as an annual plant, but it can be grown as a short-lived perennial or biennial in warmer horticultural zones with tropical or Mediterranean climates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Ocimum_basilicum_8zz.jpg/330px-Ocimum_basilicum_8zz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basil","wikidata":"Q38859"},{"title":"Pesto","blocks":[{"text":"Pesto, also known as pesto genovese, is an Italian paste traditionally made with leaves of Genovese basil, extra virgin olive oil, Parmesan, pecorino sardo, pine nuts, and garlic. It originated in the Ligurian city of Genoa and is used to dress pasta."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/BasilPesto.JPG/330px-BasilPesto.JPG?utm_source